<main>
  <h1>Leasing in Massachusetts from abroad: private landlord vs. Harvard University Housing</h1>
  <p class="sub">Housing Made EZ · Process map draft 5 (lease &amp; bank account) · Signing-from-abroad path · Private landlord vs. Harvard University Housing · October 2026</p>

  <div class="scroller" role="region" aria-label="Process map with renter, bank and landlord swim lanes; scroll horizontally">
    <div class="map" id="map">
      <div class="lane-bg r"></div><div class="lane-bg b"></div><div class="lane-bg l"></div><div class="lane-bg h"></div>
      <div class="lane-label" style="grid-row:2"><span>Renter<small>International student, still abroad until stage 10</small></span></div>
      <div class="lane-label" style="grid-row:3"><span>Bank<small>Harvard Federal Credit Union (formerly HUECU)</small></span></div>
      <div class="lane-label" style="grid-row:4"><span>Private landlord<small>or property manager / their broker</small></span></div>
      <div class="lane-label" style="grid-row:5"><span style="color:var(--huh)">Harvard University Housing<small>Alternative path: HUH as landlord. Each bubble replaces the private-market steps above it, including the student's part</small></span></div>
      <svg class="wires" id="wires" aria-hidden="true">
        <defs>
          <marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" style="fill:var(--ink)"/></marker>
          <marker id="ahu" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" style="fill:var(--unsourced)"/></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend" aria-label="Legend">
    <h2>Legend</h2>
    <div class="key"><span class="sw bad"></span><span class="fr bad">✕ Eliminate</span>Bad friction: can be removed or automated</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="sw good"></span><span class="fr good">✓ Keep</span>Good friction: payment steps worth keeping</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="sw"></span>Not marked as friction</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="sw huhlane"></span>Crimson edge = Harvard University Housing lane (identity only, not friction)</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="tag">Intl student</span>Step that differs from the standard US tenant process</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="tag huh">HUH path</span>Step when Harvard University Housing is the landlord</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="tag skip">Not needed with HUH</span>Private-market step that drops out with HUH</div>
    <div class="key"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 14"><line x1="0" y1="7" x2="32" y2="7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><polygon points="32,1 40,7 32,13" fill="currentColor"/></svg>Hand-off, delay from a source</div>
    <div class="key"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 14"><line x1="0" y1="7" x2="32" y2="7" stroke="#8f97a1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><polygon points="32,1 40,7 32,13" fill="#8f97a1"/></svg>Hand-off, delay not yet sourced</div>
    <div class="key"><span class="inferred" style="font-size:12.5px">Italic text</span>&nbsp;inferred, not from a source</div>
    <p class="note">Columns are stages: actions in the same column happen at the same time. Friction is shown by a filled box and a labeled badge, so color is never the only cue. The crimson edge and label mark the HUH lane; they don't indicate friction.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">This map follows an international student who signs a Massachusetts lease before arriving in the US, using Harvard Federal Credit Union to hold and send money. Three swim lanes show who acts: the student, the bank, and the landlord. Each column is a stage, so actions in the same column happen at the same time, and arrows show hand-offs between them with the typical delay where a source gives one. The student opens the bank account first because approval takes up to about 10 days, and searches for housing while they wait. Compared with the standard process, the student must also screen for scams, apply without an SSN or US credit, possibly pay for a guarantor, and wire money in from home. They read and understand the lease before signing it. The bottom lane shows the alternative when Harvard University Housing (HUH) is the landlord. The student applies and picks a unit in HUH's own portal, e-signs, and pays the first rent by credit card or wire, and rent is then billed to the student account. Three private-market steps drop out: the scam check, the guarantor, and funding a US bank account before signing. The trade-offs are that HUH offers no tours, units go first click, first served, and every lease ends June 30. Friction is marked in two ways. Red boxes with an “Eliminate” badge are bad friction we aim to remove or automate: the scam check and reading and understanding the lease, in both paths. Gold boxes with a “Keep” badge are good friction: every step that moves money, from wiring funds in to paying rent. Two open questions shape the private-market path: Harvard's international office warns never to wire money for a unit you haven't seen in person, which this path depends on, and it's not yet confirmed how the student can send the deposit from a savings-only account before debit cards and checks are issued at arrival.</p>

  <p class="sources">Sources:
    <a href="https://harvardfcu.org/international/">Harvard FCU — International Members</a>;
    <a href="https://www.huhousing.harvard.edu/application-process">HUH — Application Process</a>;
    <a href="https://www.huhousing.harvard.edu/application-process/FAQ">HUH — Applicant FAQ</a>;
    <a href="https://huhousing.harvard.edu/application-process/payment">HUH — Payment</a>;
    <a href="https://www.hio.harvard.edu/housing">Harvard International Office — Housing</a>;
    <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/rental-listing-scams">FTC — Rental Listing Scams</a>;
    <a href="https://www.rentcafe.com/blog/renting/know-rental-application-process/">RentCafe — Application Process</a>;
    <a href="https://www.apartments.com/rental-manager/resources/leasing-and-laws/what-landlords-should-know-about-renting-international-students">Apartments.com — Renting to International Students</a>;
    <a href="https://www.insurent.com/renters/">Insurent — Renters</a>;
    <a href="https://www.mass.gov/guides/the-attorney-generals-guide-to-landlord-and-tenant-rights">MA Attorney General — Landlord and Tenant Rights</a>;
    <a href="https://www.masslegalhelp.org/sites/default/files/2025-10/03%20Security%20Deposits%202025-%20Updated%208-13-25.pdf">MassLegalHelp — Security Deposits</a>;
    <a href="https://www.boston.gov/departments/housing/office-housing-stability/broker-fees-3-things-know-about-new-law">City of Boston — Broker Fees</a>.
  </p>
